NEARS-3450 QA finding (non-blocking, followups -- see envelope): "close via
click-on-first-vertex-marker" and "close via double-click" are both
code-correct (verified by source read: map.addListener wiring, the
fix-cycle-2 pop-twice guard, finishDrawing()'s <1-vertex abort all match
spec) but are UNRELIABLE to trigger via headless/CDP-synthesized mouse
events against this Maps version's vector (mapId + WebGL) renderer.

Repro summary (Playwright 1.60, chromium + real Chrome channel, both
headless):
  - Vertex-add clicks (map.addListener('click', ...)): reliable given
    DISTINCT pixel positions per click + a settle wait; repeat-clicking the
    SAME pixel within ~1s risks the browser's own native double-click
    detection firing and popping the just-added vertex (observed, not a
    product bug -- product's dblclick handler is doing exactly its
    documented job).
  - close-via-marker (click at the frozen startVertexMarker position,
    computed via map.getProjection() to rule out pixel mismatch): succeeded
    ~50% of repeated trials (5/11 across this session's trials); code is
    correct, target is small (SymbolPath.CIRCLE, scale:6 = 12px diameter).
  - close-via-dblclick: 0/9 successes across 3 dispatch methods
    (page.mouse.dblclick(), two page.mouse.click() 80-120ms apart, raw CDP
    Input.dispatchMouseEvent with explicit clickCount:1/2) and 2 browser
    channels (bundled Chromium, channel="chrome"). A JS-level probe
    confirmed the browser's NATIVE 'dblclick' DOM event DOES fire
    (native_dblclick=1) but google.maps.Map's own synthetic 'dblclick'
    event (the one map.addListener('dblclick', ...) subscribes to) never
    fires in response -- i.e. the miss is inside Google's own Maps JS SDK
    gesture recognizer under headless/software-WebGL, not in this ticket's
    2-file diff.
  - Directly invoking finishDrawing() (the exact function the dblclick
    handler calls) via page.evaluate with vertices=[] -- matching the
    post-pop state of a genuine zero-click double-click -- correctly sets
    drawing=false, #coordinates='', lastpolygon=null, and resets the
    control label to idle. This confirms the abort guard itself, live, on
    the shipped code, independent of the gesture-dispatch problem above.

Conclusion: not filed as a task_bug (breaks_ac: false) because (a) the
underlying application code is correct by inspection and by the one path
that IS exercisable (finishDrawing() invoked directly), (b) one of the two
documented close gestures (click-on-marker) DOES work when the synthetic
click precisely lands, satisfying the AC's "OR", and (c) the failure
signature (native DOM event fires, Maps' internal recognizer doesn't) is
squarely inside Google's third-party SDK under headless/CDP automation, a
well-documented category of Maps+headless flakiness, not something this
2-file diff could cause or fix. Logged as a non-blocking followup:
re-verify the double-click-close gesture with a real physical mouse in a
headed browser before closing the loop completely.
